<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swabian Circle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1500">1500</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swabian Circle was one of the Imperial Circles into which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> was divided from 1500 to organise defence, coinage and peace-keeping. It covered south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and was the most fragmented of all, with nearly a hundred members, including Württemberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, prince-bishoprics, imperial abbeys, counts and over thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a>. It was among the most active circles until 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swabian-circle.jpg" alt="Swabian Circle" width="320" height="313" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Swabian_Circle.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
